# CareLoop local configuration — TEMPLATE. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is ignored by git (`.gitignore` ignores `.env` and `.env.*`, and negates this file
# alone). Verified with `git check-ignore -v .env`, not by reading the pattern. Never commit a
# populated copy, and never paste a key into a conversation, an issue or a commit message.
#
# Nothing under `careloop/` or `tests/` reads this file. The default test suite runs entirely on
# the mock model provider, at $0, with zero secrets configured — CI proves it, since CI has none.
#
# Two entry points load it, and they load it differently — which matters for anything that boto3
# resolves rather than CareLoop:
#
#   bench/run.py             reads it into a MAPPING and passes that down. The process environment
#                            is untouched, so boto3 never sees these values; only the variables
#                            careloop/llm/providers.py reads out of the mapping itself take effect.
#   scripts/demo_serve.py    reads it into the PROCESS environment, for keys not already set, so
#                            boto3's own credential chain does see them.
#
# What this file is NOT:
#
#   deploy/careloop.env      the deployed application's configuration, on the instance
#   your shell / ~/.aws      deployment authentication. `scripts/deploy.py` takes its identity from
#                            the AWS CLI/SDK profile in your shell and reads nothing here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS — Bedrock (Nova Lite, Nova Micro)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Use a DEDICATED IAM USER for this, not root credentials and not an admin key. The benchmark
# needs exactly three actions, and an inline policy granting only these is enough:
#
#   bedrock:InvokeModel             the actual model calls
#   bedrock:ListFoundationModels    enumerating what the account can reach
#   sts:GetCallerIdentity           confirming which principal is in use before spending
#
# Root credentials cannot be scoped, cannot be rotated without disruption, and cannot be revoked
# in isolation if they leak. A dedicated user can be deleted outright.
#
# Region matters: Bedrock model availability is per-region, and the model id below is a
# region-prefixed inference profile (see CARELOOP_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID).
#
# `AWS_REGION` is checked first and `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION` second, so one exported in your shell
# wins over the one set here. There is deliberately no `AWS_REGION=` blank below: two spellings of
# one setting in a template is a way to set them to different things.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1

# Only for temporary credentials (STS / SSO / assumed role). Leave blank for a long-lived IAM
# user key pair. If set, it expires — a probe that worked yesterday failing today with
# ExpiredTokenException means this, not a quota change.
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

# OPTIONAL, and an alternative to the key pair above rather than an addition to it. Names a
# profile in your AWS CLI/SDK configuration (`~/.aws/config`, `~/.aws/credentials`) for the local
# provider path to authenticate with.
#
# How it takes effect, precisely, because it is not symmetric with the variables above.
# `careloop/llm/providers.py::bedrock_session` builds an explicit boto3 session ONLY when it finds
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY; with neither, it returns None and boto3's own
# credential chain takes over — and that chain is what reads a profile. So:
#
#   scripts/demo_serve.py    works. It loads this file into the process environment, which is
#                            where boto3 looks.
#   bench/run.py             does NOT. It passes a mapping boto3 never sees, so a profile named
#                            here reaches nothing. Export it in your shell for a benchmark run.
#
# `careloop-deployer` is NOT the value to put here. That principal deploys — S3, SSM, STS — and
# has no Bedrock permission at all; a benchmark profile is a separate identity with the three
# actions listed above. Deployment authentication does not come from this file in either
# direction: `scripts/deploy.py` reads no `.env` and takes its identity from your shell.
#
# No credential, token, account id or machine-specific CA setting belongs in this file, whichever
# way it is authenticated.
#
# One caution, because setting this makes it likelier. The test suite must never reach a real
# credential, and CI guarantees that by pointing AWS_CONFIG_FILE and AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE at
# /nonexistent with AWS_EC2_METADATA_DISABLED=true. A developer machine has no such isolation, so a
# profile configured in ~/.aws can be picked up by boto3 during a local run. If a provider test
# fails locally with a credential error and passes in CI, export those three the way
# .github/workflows/ci.yml does before running pytest.
AWS_PROFILE=

# Full override of the Bedrock model id. Bedrock uses region-prefixed inference-profile ids
# (`us.` / `eu.` / `apac.`); the bare `amazon.nova-lite-v1:0` fails on accounts that require a
# profile. Leave blank to let providers.py derive the prefix from the resolved region.
CARELOOP_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenAI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This key holds a small, non-replaceable balance. `bench/run.py` prints an estimated cost and
# refuses to make a paid call without an explicit --confirm-spend flag; responses are cached to
# disk so re-running an unchanged task set costs nothing.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NVIDIA — hosted models via the OpenAI-compatible path
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same provider class as OpenAI, pointed at a different base_url. Keys come from build.nvidia.com.
NVIDIA_API_KEY=
NVIDIA_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model selection per role
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which model backs each role. Unset means the mock provider, which is the default everywhere and
# is the only model the test suite ever sees. Set these only to point CareLoop at a real endpoint.
#
# Spec form:  mock                        the scripted mock provider
#             bedrock                     model id derived from the region prefix
#             bedrock:<model-id>          explicit model id
#             openai:<model>              OpenAI proper, key from OPENAI_API_KEY
#             openai:<model>@<base_url>   any OpenAI-compatible gateway, key from OPENAI_API_KEY
#             nvidia:<model>              NVIDIA hosted, key from NVIDIA_API_KEY, base from
#                                         NVIDIA_BASE_URL
#
# `nvidia` is the same OpenAI-compatible provider class with a different credential source. It
# gets its own prefix rather than being written as `openai:<model>@<nvidia url>` because the key
# to use would then have to be guessed from the host, and a benchmark that silently sends the
# wrong key produces an auth error that reads like a model failure.
#
# Examples:   CARELOOP_MODEL_TRIAGE=bedrock:us.amazon.nova-micro-v1:0
#             CARELOOP_MODEL_PLANNER=openai:gpt-4o-mini
#             CARELOOP_MODEL_EXTRACTION=nvidia:meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct
#
# An unrecognised spec raises at resolution. It does NOT fall back to the mock: a run that looks
# real, costs nothing and proves nothing is the worst available failure mode.
#
# MEASURED DEFAULTS, from docs/model-benchmark.md (2026-08-11, 20 tasks, 6 candidates):
#
#   CARELOOP_MODEL_TRIAGE=bedrock:us.amazon.nova-micro-v1:0
#   CARELOOP_MODEL_PLANNER=bedrock:us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0
#   CARELOOP_MODEL_EXTRACTION=bedrock:us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0
#
# Note what is NOT recommended: `bedrock:us.amazon.nova-lite-v1:0`. It was the intended primary
# until it was measured. Given an explicit booking instruction alongside a document asking for
# something else, it proposed emailing the document's address instead. The policy engine denies
# that call, but there is no reason to pick the model that makes it.
#
# `openai:gpt-4o-mini` is the fallback and is within noise of Nova 2 Lite on planning.
#
# Left blank below on purpose: the library defaults to the mock, and the test suite must never see
# anything else. Set them in your own .env when running the real system.
CARELOOP_MODEL_TRIAGE=
CARELOOP_MODEL_PLANNER=
CARELOOP_MODEL_EXTRACTION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo World — the external clinic careloop/tools/clinic.py books appointments with
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Run it in its own process, because it is meant to be a system CareLoop does not own:
#
#   python -m demoworld --port 8100
#   python scripts/demo_serve.py --clinic-url http://127.0.0.1:8100 --port 8000
#
# Blank means every effect stays simulated and a verification honestly reports `read_back: false`.
# Set it and `book_appointment` becomes a real HTTP call whose result is verified by reading the
# clinic back. No credential is involved: Demo World is synthetic, holds no PHI, and authenticates
# nobody — the same reason `X-Caregiver` is a 400 rather than a 401.
CARELOOP_CLINIC_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Amazon S3 — the second real external effect
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The exact counterpart of CARELOOP_CLINIC_URL, for the other effect that reaches a real provider.
# Blank means every `upload_document` stays simulated, exactly as every booking does without a
# clinic URL. Set it to a private bucket and the upload becomes a real create-once `PutObject`
# whose result is verified by fetching the object's bytes back and digesting them here.
#
# Read by `scripts/demo_serve.py` as the default for `--upload-bucket`. Credentials are boto3's
# ambient chain — the key pair above, or AWS_PROFILE, or an instance role — and no bucket policy,
# key or ARN belongs in this file. The measured provider contract is in
# docs/objectstore-contract.md; what has to exist in AWS is in docs/deployment-objectstore.md.
#
# This is NOT the deployment artefact bucket. That one is named in deploy/README.md, is written by
# `scripts/deploy.py` from your shell's credentials, and never appears here.
CARELOOP_UPLOAD_BUCKET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Serving a built frontend from the same origin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Read by `scripts/demo_serve.py` and `scripts/e2e_serve.py` as the default for `--static-dir`.
# Blank serves the API alone, which is what you want when Vite is serving the UI on its own port.
# Point it at `frontend/dist` after `npm --prefix frontend run build` to reproduce the deployed
# single-origin shape locally — which is the only arrangement where a session header travelling
# with the shell can be exercised without a CDN.
CARELOOP_STATIC_DIR=
